{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Optimize the dense DP baseline for the likely small/moderate-N regime: use a contiguous byte blocked representation and a single in-place DP row (dp[j]=blocked?0:(dp[j]+left)%MOD), fast buffered input, and reduce modulo with conditional subtraction. Preserve all endpoint/block semantics and benchmark against baseline.\",\"B\":\"Research a correct sparse-obstacle alternative: sort blocked cells by coordinates and use binomial path counts plus inclusion-exclusion over predecessor obstacles, with factorial/inverse-factorial precomputation modulo MOD. Establish a safe runtime crossover versus dense DP; handle duplicate blocks and blocked start/end.\",\"C\":\"Investigate practical 4-core parallelization of dense DP without changing results, preferably cache-friendly tiled/wavefront scheduling with bounded synchronization. Compare it honestly to the optimized sequential row DP; retain it only if measurements show a gain on this target.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only independently correct candidates. Build a hybrid that selects sparse combinatorial DP or optimized dense DP from input dimensions/obstacle count using measured crossover, validate randomized small cases against the original, and measure the final integrated program since gains are not assumed additive.\",\"analysis\":\"Problem is grid-path counting modulo 1e9+7. The current O(N^2) two-row DP is correct but vector<vector<bool>> and duplicated rows add overhead, while N may be very large. Dense one-row DP is the low-risk improvement; sparse combinatorial inclusion-exclusion can avoid scanning an enormous grid when obstacle count is small, but is quadratic in obstacles and therefore requires a measured hybrid threshold. Parallel wavefront work has dependency/synchronization risk and must earn its complexity experimentally. Baseline is valid at 0.0810 s on the provided measurement.\"}"}}
